                 Digital Widens 64-Bit Server Lead  
   with New Midrange and Enhanced Enterprise AlphaServer Systems,
           Performance Records, Data Warehouse Milestones 


MAYNARD, Mass. -- September 4, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today widened its lead in the commercial and technical 64-bit server 
market by unveiling new AlphaServer midrange systems, significant 
enhancements to enterprise-class AlphaServer systems, industry-
leading TPC-C and TPC-D results, and Digital's 200th data warehouse 
sale. 

     "Today's announcements reinforce Digital's ability to deliver 
to our customers industry-leading performance together with the best 
value and investment protection across the entire AlphaServer line," 
said Pauline Nist, vice president of the AlphaServer Business 
Segment. "Our key competitors have barely begun shipping their 
first-generation 64-bit hardware. The 64-bit AlphaServer solution is 
complete, battle-tested and mainstream. Today's news widens the gap 
between Digital and its nearest rival." 

The New AlphaServer 4000 Midrange Systems

     As the newest member of the AlphaServer 4000 midrange family, the 
AlphaServer 4000 5/300 and AlphaServer 4000 5/400 systems provide cost-
effective, high-performance, fully scalable,  cluster-ready 64-bit platforms.  
The new systems are ideally suited for business-critical applications such as
high-capacity data marts, high-availability  OLTP, enterprise E-mail, highly
scalable PC LAN and internetworking,  and communications and telephony.

     AlphaServer 4000 systems feature the same exclusive system 
drawer building block approach that Digital introduced in May with 
the AlphaServer 4100 platform. The AlphaServer 4100 platform is the 
first midrange server to support 64-bit very large memory (VLM64). A 
choice of pedestal or small-footprint cabinet models provide 
mix-and-match, rack-and-stack flexibility for meeting customers' 
current and future business and technology needs. Users can start 
with a pedestal, expand by adding a cabinet, and add additional 
system drawers to the cabinet. 

     The new systems offer up to two 300-MHz or 400-MHz Alpha 21164 
processors and up to 2 Gbytes of memory today and 4 Gbytes starting 
in November. AlphaServer 4100 systems will support up to 8 Gbytes of 
memory in November. In addition, all AlphaServer 4000 and 4100 
systems now ship with a larger, 4.3 Gbyte Fast Wide SCSI-2 disk and 
controller, and Fast Ethernet. 

     AlphaServer 4000 systems also feature 64-bit PCI dual-channel, 
eight-slot I/O subsystem to ensure balanced system performance and 
to take advantage of industry-standard PCI options. For flexibility 
and investment protection, the new 128-bit wide, 1.1 GB synchronous 
system bus is "speed-matched" to the processor, enabling it to grow 
in bandwidth as processor speeds increase across the AlphaServer 
4000  product line.

     For even greater levels of availability, performance, and 
affordability, the system can be clustered with any other 
AlphaServer system, such as the AlphaServer 1000A, 4100, or 8400 
systems, using Digital's standard-setting OpenVMS Clusters, UNIX 
TruCluster Solutions, or Windows NT clustering capabilities. 

     AlphaServer 4000 5/300 systems are available from Digital and 
its business partners and are priced starting at $34,500, with a 
single 300-MHz processor, Windows NT, 128 MB memory, and 4.3 Gbytes 
disk. Fully configured 400-MHz systems are available September 14, 
1996, priced from $44,500. 

     AlphaServer 4000 systems are fully upgradable to the more 
powerful, more expandable AlphaServer 4100 line, with support for up 
to four processors and 4 Gbytes of memory. AlphaServer 4000 to 4100 
system upgrade prices begin at $22,500. In addition, AlphaServer 
4000 5/300 and AlphaServer 4100 5/300 systems, both running at 300 
MHz, can be upgraded to 400-MHz systems, with prices starting at 
$10,000. 

Industry-Leading Database and Application Performance

     Offering record-breaking midrange TPC-C results of 7,598 
transactions per minute (tpmC) at $152/tpmC, the AlphaServer 4100 
5/400 running Sybase SQL Server System 11 and Digital UNIX is now 
the top-performing four-processor UNIX SMP server on the market.  
These results, based on the industry-standard TPC-C database 
performance benchmark, give the AlphaServer 4100 a significant 
competitive advantage over it's three closest competitors: Sun 
UltraEnterprise E3000, HP 9000 K420, and IBM RS/6000 J40.

     Using four processors and 3 Gbytes of memory, the AlphaServer 
system beats Sun's UltraEnterprise E3000 performance by more than 
900 tpmC with two fewer processors, at the same price/performance. 
The AlphaServer system outperforms HP's K420 by more than 50% with 
25% better price/performance, and provides 31% higher performance 
with 28% percent better price/performance than the IBM J40.  

     In addition, AlphaServer systems provide industry-leading 
application performance, including:

    * Microsoft Exchange -- 3,600 users, using an AlphaServer 4000 5/400 
      with Windows NT.

    * Platinum Accounting -- 132,890 APVEs/hr., using AlphaServer 4100 
      5/300 with 4 processors, 512 MB of memory, Windows NT.

    * SPECweb96 Internet server -- 974 operations per second, using 
      AlphaServer 4000 with 2 processors, 1 Gbyte memory, Zeus Internet 
      Server V1.0.1.

    * AlphaServer 8000 Family: Faster, More Memory, Lower Cost

      "When we introduced the AlphaServer 8000 enterprise server 
family in April 1995, Digital immediately grabbed the lead in high-
end computing performance and value. And we haven't let go since," 
said Nist. "Today we are again raising the bar by dramatically 
increasing performance while holding prices at or below current 
levels."

     Digital's top-of-the-line AlphaServer 8400, Datamation's 
"Server of the Year," and the AlphaServer 8200 are designed for 
customers whose business success depends on successful, affordable 
data warehousing, decision support, enterprise resource planning 
(ERP), or scientific processing. These economical, mainframe-class 
systems are now available with 12 and six, respectively, of the 
world's fastest processors, the Alpha 21164A, running at 437 MHz. 

     In addition, a new 4-Gbyte memory option will dramatically 
increase system capacity, performance, and efficiency. Available in 
January and priced at less than $100/MB, this option doubles the 
memory capacity of each system, to 28 Gbytes in the AlphaServer 8400 
5/350 or 5/440, and 12 Gbytes in AlphaServer 8200 systems. 

     The new AlphaServer 8000 5/440 systems deliver about 25% 
greater integer and commercial performance than the 5/350 model, 15% 
greater floating point performance, at equivalent or lower prices.

     In addition, for greater performance and efficiency, 
AlphaServer 8400 customers can now take even fuller advantage of 
Alpha's superior scaling. Customers can configure 10 processors and 
12 Gbytes of memory in a single system using Digital UNIX or 
OpenVMS.

     AlphaServer 8000 5/440 systems are available now. Pricing 
starts at $155,000 for a dual-CPU AlphaServer 8200 5/440 with 128 MB 
of memory, 2.1-Gbyte disk. This is $10,000 less than 5/350 models. 
AlphaServer 8400 5/440 systems are priced beginning at $297,000 with 
256 MB of memory and 2.1-Gbyte disk. Installation is included, as it 
is for all 5/440 systems. 

     In addition, the company reduced prices for selected entry-
level AlphaServer 8200 5/300 systems, memory, and SMP processor 
boards, by as much as 20%. This enables users to build powerful, 
large-scale AlphaServer 8000 system configurations at significantly 
lower cost. Effective immediately, 5/440 SMP upgrades are reduced by 
up to 62%, or $65,000 for each dual-CPU 4/440 module and $40,000 for 
each 5/300 dual-CPU add-on module.

World-record TPC-D Benchmark Results 

     Digital also announced the world's best single-system TPC-D 
benchmark results for the new AlphaServer 8400 5/440 enterprise 
system, powered by 12 437-MHz Alpha processors running Digital UNIX 
and Oracle7. Based on the TPC-D 100-Gbyte database, a single 
AlphaServer 8400 5/440 system eclipses the previous record with 
TPC-D Power rating of 864.3 QppD, Throughput of 445.4 QphD, and 
Price/performance of $2,170.37/QphD.

     "Yet another industry-standard measurement helps us demonstrate 
the dominant power, scaling, and affordability of the AlphaServer 
platform," said Nist.

     Compared with the HP 9000 EPS30, the AlphaServer results are 
360% better on both power and throughput, with price/performance 
one-sixth the HP figure.  Only the NCR 5100M offers higher power and 
throughput, but using a 20-node, 160-processor cluster, and at 
price/performance more than six times higher than the AlphaServer 
system.

     TPC-D is the industry-standard benchmark for measuring system 
performance and price/performance for a decision support environment 
in which complex ad hoc business-oriented queries are submitted 
against a large database. 

Leadership Data Warehouse Solutions 

     Digital has become the fastest-growing supplier of complete 
data warehouse solutions. In just 15 months, the company has sold 
more than 200 Alpha Warehouse data warehouse solutions, built on the 
power of the AlphaServer 8000 platform and applications from leading 
software and systems integration providers.  

     Wyeth-Ayerst Laboratories, a division of American Home 
Products, received the 200th AlphaServer 8000 for data warehouse 
applications. Wyeth-Ayerst selected Digital's AlphaServer 8200 with 
four processors running Digital UNIX, Oracle Corporation's Oracle7 
relational database, and software applications and services from SIA 
Corporation. 

     With performance and price as critical elements of the decision 
making process, it was clear after a competitive benchmark that 
Digital's AlphaServer 8200 would provide the foundation of 
Wyeth-Ayerst's data warehouse and Digital's TruCluster technology 
would provide the scalability and growth path.  When fully 
implemented, Wyeth-Ayerst expects the Alpha Warehouse solution to 
reduce turnaround on business queries by a factor of 20. This will 
clearly provide the business units with the decision making tools 
they need to compete effectively in a very dynamic industry.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/ 
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 

