           Thinking Machines Brings Darwin Data Mining Software 
                     to Digital's Alpha Platform


Solution brings large ROI applications to AlphaServer environments.


BEDFORD, Mass., September 4, 1996 --  Thinking Machines Corporation
today announced its Darwin suite of data mining software will be
available for Digital Equipment Corporation's 64-bit AlphaServer
family, running either Digital UNIX or Windows NT. Darwin's suite of
data mining software enables businesses to analyze huge databases to
discover new patterns, extract hidden information, and predict future
trends.

Darwin will be available for the Digital AlphaServer platforms in the
fourth quarter of this year and will be jointly marketed by Digital
and Thinking Machines. The availability of Darwin on Alpha reinforces
Thinking Machines' role as a provider of high-end data mining
solutions to the commercial market via the world's most powerful
workstation and server platforms.

Leaders Join for Superior Data Mining Performance

Darwin's unique parallel software architecture enables it to scale
easily to accommodate very large databases (VLDBs). By dividing the
data mining process into simultaneous operations, Darwin slashes the
time required to yield meaningful results. Models are built faster,
while large volumes of data can be processed in a fraction of the time
of sequential systems. Because of its ability to address sophisticated
strategic applications, Darwin is currently being evaluated by many
companies spanning a variety of industries, including retail sites,
financial services and credit card companies, entertainment companies,
and banking organizations.

"Having Darwin available on Digital's AlphaServer platform represents
a major milestone for Thinking Machines," said Alan L. Codkind, vice
president, marketing, Thinking Machines. "The AlphaServer family is
the leading platform for high-performance 64-bit computing. When combined 
with the Digital Alpha Warehouse, Darwin will really churn through the data 
to expose hidden relationships. The net result for our customers will be
increased productivity, improved model quality and decreased time to market."

"Darwin is an excellent addition to Digital's portfolio of data
warehousing solutions," said Mike Gallup, vice president of worldwide
systems marketing, Digital Equipment Corp. "With solutions for both
64-bit Digital UNIX and Windows NT on Alpha, Darwin users can take
advantage of the tremendous scalability of the AlphaServer family.
Darwin allows customers to use the speed and power of Alpha to derive
business insights from their huge databases, and access information
for strategic advantage."

Data Mining - A Rapidly Growing Market

Interest in data mining and knowledge discovery has been building
because of the competitive opportunity inherent in analyzing the ever
increasing amounts of data accumulating in corporate databases and
data warehouses.  In fact, according to the Meta Group, of Stamford
Conn., the data mining market in the US will reach $300 million in
1997 and rise to $800 million by the year 2000.

About the Digital AlphaServer Family

Digital's AlphaServer family of systems is the industry's only
complete line of high-performance 64-bit RISC servers, ranging from
the AlphaServer 8000 enterprise servers at the high end, to the
departmental and network AlphaServer 4000 and 2000 class systems, to
the workgroup and LAN AlphaServer 400 platform. Digital offers a
balanced choice of processor speed; single and multi-processor
symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) configurations; 64-bit Digital UNIX
and OpenVMS operating systems, Windows NT operating system; clustering
and other high availability features; very large memory; 64-bit
database support by Informix, Oracle, Software AG, and Sybase; and
thousands of the most popular applications.

Digital's Alpha Warehouse combines industry leading open hardware and
operating systems, best-in-class software applications, and system
integration capabilities to transform technology into business
benefits. Only Digital provides the complete 64-bit environment Q
processor, operating system, and applications Q all available today.


About Darwin

Darwin is a scalable data mining application that finds subtle and
often elusive relationships among billions of pieces of data and uses
this new information to help solve a variety of business problems,
from marketing challenges to quality management to manufacturing
efficiency.

Darwin is able to mine even the largest of data warehouses, due to its
parallel processing design. It can run on a wide range of hardware
architectures, including standalone and networked workstations, SMPs,
and clusters of each. In addition, Darwin's multi-algorithm strategy
(i.e., neural networks, decision trees, nearest neighbor matching, and
genetic algorithms) ensures maximum flexibility, accuracy and
performance.

Darwin can access any relational database through its ODBC compliant
front end, including Informix, Oracle, and Sybase. Darwin can also
work with a variety of data visualization tools for displaying model
results.

Pricing for Darwin begins at $50,000 and is based on the number of
processors in the configuration.

Founded in 1983, Thinking Machines revolutionized high-performance
computing with its massively parallel supercomputing technology.
Today Thinking Machines employs over 185 people who produce, market
and sell software applications and development tools for computer
cluster technology. The company's powerful Darwin data mining software
is available on multiple platforms.  With offices worldwide, Thinking
Machines is headquartered in Bedford, Massachusetts.
