         Digital's 2000th AlphaServer 8000 Enterprise System
                   to Help Power Netscape WWW Site


   ... Netscape employs Digital's 2,000th, 64-bit AlphaServer 8000
            to help handle Netscape Web site traffic ...


   ... More than 35 percent of AlphaServer 8000's shipped include
                 Very-Large-Memory capabilities ...


MAYNARD, Mass., September 4, 1996 -- Netscape Communications 
Corporation today became the recipient of the 2,000th AlphaServer 
8000 enterprise system to be shipped by Digital Equipment 
Corporation. Netscape is using a high-performance, 64-bit 
AlphaServer 8400 system to help power its site on the World Wide 
Web, to meet the heavy demand of users accessing the site and also 
downloading the latest version of Netscape's Internet client 
software -- Netscape Navigator 3.0. Netscape's site is the busiest 
site on the Web, receiving over 80 million hits a day.

     The AlphaServer 8400 enterprise system, which is running 64-bit 
Digital UNIX -- is being used by Netscape to assist customers who 
are requesting information from its Web site 
(http://www.netscape.com).  The AlphaServer 8400 system is capable 
of supporting up to 12 350-MHz Alpha processors and 14 gigabytes of 
memory. The AlphaServer is highly scalable and therefore offers 
Netscape -- and other customers -- enormous levels of growth 
potential.

     "In the past six months, traffic on the Netscape Web site has 
more than doubled, growing from 40 million to more than 80 million 
'hits' a day," said Mike Homer, senior vice president of marketing 
at Netscape. "Digital's AlphaServer 8400 brings us some of the most 
powerful technology available to help support this explosive growth.   
We are looking forward to using 64-bit computing power to enable us 
to provide even higher levels of performance and customer 
satisfaction on the Netscape Web site."

     "Digital has shipped more than $10 billion in Alpha systems 
revenue, a milestone that proves 64-bit computing is mainstream.  
We're extremely pleased that Netscape, like many others Digital 
customers, has installed the AlphaServer system," said Mike Gallup, 
Digital's worldwide vice president of systems marketing.  

     "Many of our competitors have announced their 64-bit plans, and 
have begun only recently to deliver pieces of what constitutes a 
complete 64-bit computing environments," Gallup added. "Digital 
provides the complete 64-bit computing solution - architecture, 
operating systems, and applications through our partners."

     "Also what is very exciting is that more than 35 percent of 
AlphaServer 8000 systems sold-to-date support Digital's exclusive 
64-bit very-large-memory (VLM64) technology, which offers data 
access up to 20,000 times faster than access to data on disk, for 
significant performance gains and cost savings," he said.

     Digital's 64-bit very large memory (VLM64) technology 
dramatically improves database and application performance by 
raising the amount of information that can be stored in memory.  
While 32-bit systems are limited to 2 Gbytes of memory, AlphaServer 
8400 systems can store up to 14 Gbytes of data in memory.

     Designed to appeal to a broad range of Internet users, the 
Netscape site provides in-depth information targeted at specific 
groups, including corporate users, consumers and developers building 
applications for the Netscape platform. The site also provides 
Netscape product information, case studies on Intranet applications 
from Fortune 500 companies, technology white papers, interactive 
product demonstrations and a Netscape product roadmap help meet 
corporate users' needs for up-to-date Internet information and 
intranet application examples. For consumers, the "Netscape Search" 
button gives them rapid access to popular search and directory 
services and the "What's New" and "What's Cool" listings keep Net 
surfers abreast of late-breaking Internet trends.

     Netscape's 13,000 person-strong developer community uses the 
DevEdge Online Netscape site to access detailed product and 
technical information, online support services, beta versions of 
Netscape software essential for their product development work and 
transcripts of presentations from the Netscape Developer Conference.

Growing AlphaServer Market Momentum

     In addition to Netscape, companies in virtually every industry 
and in 100 countries are implementing Alpha solutions.  These 
include Best Western Hotels, Cerner Corporation, Ben & Jerry's, 
Oracle, Lycos, Rhone Poulenc Rorer, Baxter Healthcare, LSI Logic, 
New York Mercantile Exchange, Northrop Grumman Corp., Worcester 
Polytechnic Institute, Schering Plough, Reuters Information 
Technology, NOMA Industries, Guaranteed Overnight Delivery, British 
Telecom, Short Brothers PLC, Hebrew University, Telecom Italia 
Mobile, Banco Nacional de Credito, Lisbon, Swedish Postal Service, 
Swiss Telecom PTT, Aerospatiale, Sumitomo Bank, and Royal Hong Kong 
Jockey Club, as well as government agencies such as the Department 
of Defense, U.S. Air Force and Army, NASA, and the British Royal 
Navy.

     "Because Digital is the 64-bit volume leader, ISVs want to work 
with us.  Today, more than 8,000 applications from leading 
third-party software suppliers run on Alpha, and that number is 
growing every day," said Gallup. "And because the Alpha architecture 
is battle-tested -- not first-generation 64-bit technology -- our 
customers are guaranteed a secure, stable and robust computing 
environment."

Other Alpha-based business highlights include: 

     *	In the quarter ended June 30, Alpha system revenue grew by 
        34 percent year-over-year, driven by demand for the new 
        AlphaServer 4100 mid-range system and the AlphaServer 8000 
        enterprise server family; 

     *	In the same quarter, AlphaServer 8000 sales grew by 84 
        percent; 

     *	Year-over-year, Alpha systems revenue growth exceeded 45 
        percent; 

     *	Over the same period, Digital UNIX server revenues are up 84 
        percent, and 

     *	Sales of Alpha-based Windows NT servers soared by more than 
        100 percent over the previous fiscal year.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
