        Digital Continues Assault on Visual Computing Market
                   with New 3D Graphics Investment


   ...Megatek agreement to build on Digital's status as Windows NT
                   workstation graphics leader...


MAYNARD, Mass., September 10, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation
today announced plans to acquire from Nihon Unisys Limited (NUL),
of Tokyo, Japan, and its subsidiary -- Megatek -- Megatek's 3D 
graphics technology, and to employ its 3D graphics engineering team.

     Under the terms of the memorandum of understanding, Digital and 
NUL also will collaborate on graphics engineering projects. 
Specifically, Digital will work with NUL to complete development of 
NUL products targeted for customers in Japan.  Digital will expand 
its own research and development operations by occupying Megatek's 
facilities in San Diego, Calif. 

     Megatek, which is based in San Diego, was founded in 1972 and 
is a leader in development of graphics devices with advanced visual 
quality. It was acquired by NUL in 1992 to participate in joint 
development of a 3D graphics accelerator targeting the Japanese 
automotive market. Megatek's products are widely used in mechanical 
design and manufacturing applications and other manufacturing 
industries. 

     "Our plans to acquire Megatek's 3D technology and to employ its 
engineering staff are the latest in a recent series of actions taken 
to fortify our leadership position in 3D graphics, and will result 
in quick delivery of new graphics products delivering unrivaled 
visual quality and performance to our Windows NT and UNIX 
customers," said Philippe Ribeyre, vice president of Digital's 
Workstation Business Segment.  "Our planned acquisition from NUL is 
part of our overall 3D graphics strategy, and shows our commitment 
to 3D graphics as a significant component of structure and strategic 
plan." 

     Using Megatek's patented graphics technology, Digital will 
deliver graphics accelerators displaying visually-precise computer 
models thereby eliminating a customer's need to produce expensive 
prototype models and reducing time-to-market and development costs 
in the automotive, entertainment, aerospace and consumer electronics 
markets. 

Megatek announcement follows stream of Digital visual 
computing investments

     The NUL acquisition will cap Digital's stream of recent major 
workstation announcements, further solidifying the company's 
leadership position in the visual computing arena and in the growing 
market for advanced Windows NT-based 3D graphics.

     "Digital is fine-tuning its workstation strategy to focus on 
the rapidly increasing market opportunities in the Windows NT 
space," said Ribeyre.  "It is our intent to be the leading provider 
of Windows NT-based workstations, with visual computing solutions as 
our key differentiator." Recent visual computing solution announcements 
by Digital include the following:

     - 	In August, Digital extended its PowerStorm graphics adapter 
        family -- the PowerStorm 4D40T, 4D50T and 4D60T.  The 4DT 
        series are the world's fastest Windows NT 3D graphics 
        accelerators, and on UNIX systems, rival SGI's Maximum 
        IMPACT, at a fraction of the price;

     - 	Also last month, Digital and Evans & Sutherland, a leading 
        manufacturer of hardware and software for highly realistic 
        visual systems, announced the two companies will collaborate 
        to deliver advanced high-end 3D graphics on Digital's 
        Alpha-based Windows NT workstations;

     - 	To build on its Windows NT graphics leadership position, 
        Digital established a graphics software engineering center 
        in Bellevue, Wash., close to nearby Redmond;

     - 	Earlier this year, Digital debuted the first members of the 
        PowerStorm family of PCI-based workstation graphics options, 
        which deliver exceptional midrange 2D and 3D performance, at 
        affordable prices. 

     Digital's workstation graphics technology targets technical 
professionals in mechanical and electronic design automation, 
entertainment, science and research and geographical information 
systems, among other graphics-intensive applications areas.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
