            Digital and EDS Sign Agreement for Support of
        DECnet, Wide Area Networking, and DECwindows Products


MAYNARD, Mass., September 10, 1996 - Digital Equipment Corporation 
and Electronic Data Systems (EDS) today announced the signing of a 
long-term engineering support agreement through which EDS will 
provide support for selected Digital OpenVMS and Digital UNIX 
network connectivity products.

     Digital will continue to drive business management and 
engineering direction for these products -  as well as all sales, 
marketing, and service.

     Under terms of the agreement, EDS will provide engineering 
support for Digital's DECnet/OSI and Wide Area Networking (X.25, 
ISDN) software products. These products provide network connectivity 
for OpenVMS and Digital UNIX systems, and enhance Digital's overall 
networking strategy. In addition, EDS will provide support for 
Digital's OpenVMS DECwindows product.

     Products covered by this agreement are:

     * 	DECnet VAX
     * 	DECnet/OSI for OpenVMS
     * 	DECnet/OSI for Digital UNIX
     * 	X.25 for OpenVMS
     * 	Wide Area Networking Support for Digital UNIX (X.25 and 
        ISDN)
     * 	X.25 for OpenVMS Alpha Systems           
     * 	DECwindows for OpenVMS Systems and Graphical X Server 
        Software

     "This agreement underscores Digital's on-going commitment to 
meet customer demands for leadership support of its network 
connectivity products, and further demonstrates Digital's intent to 
establish agreements with leading vendors to offer customers the 
best solutions for their business needs," said Rich Marcello, vice 
president of Digital's OpenVMS Systems Software Group.

     "Digital values the expertise that EDS brings to our family of 
network connectivity products," Marcello noted, "We look forward to 
working with EDS to ensure that our customers receive the best 
product support, and that the investments they have made in 
equipment, software, and training are protected."

     Digital and EDS have had an ongoing relationship for several 
years to provide customers with quality product support - initially 
for the IBM Interconnect and DECset software products through an 
earlier agreement announced in 1995.

     Kevin Malloy, director of EDS' Advanced Software Group said 
"this agreement is another significant milestone in the growing 
software engineering partnership between EDS and Digital based on 
the mutual trust which has developed from the proven success of the 
flagship DECset and IBM Interconnect contracts.

     "It is important to EDS because it expands our capability in 
networking and related software which now underpins so many of the 
enterprise-wide business systems of our customers," Malloy added.

     EDS (NYSE & LSE:EDS) is a leader in the global information 
services industry. The company's more than 95,000 employees 
specialize in applying a range of ideas and technologies to help 
business and government customers improve their economics, products, 
services and customer relationships.  EDS, which serves customers in 
42 countries, reported revenues of $12.4 billion in 1995. EDS' Europe, 
Middle East and Africa group employs more than 19,000 people in 23 
countries and reported revenues of $2.4 billion in 1995. EDS can be 
visited via the Internet at http://www.eds.com.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
