        SARAW Computer-Based Literacy Program Wins 
        Governor General's Flight for Freedom Award


 ... Speech Assisted Reading and Writing (SARAW) program 
 launched in partnership with Digital Equipment of Canada ...


The Neil Squire Foundation's Speech Assisted Reading and Writing 
(SARAW) program has been awarded The Governor General's Flight for 
Freedom Award for its development of a nationwide, computer-assisted 
literacy program designed to help Canadians with physical 
disabilities. The program, developed in partnership with Digital 
Equipment of Canada and Vancouver-based Capilano College, was 
developed as a nationwide literacy initiative that has helped 
hundreds of Canadians with disabilities access literacy education.

For many adults with physical disabilities who are unable to hold a 
pencil or turn pages in a book, SARAW is a talking computer program 
that offers users a chance to learn basic reading and writing 
skills. The software can be used by students even if their physical 
disabilities make it impossible for them to use a standard computer 
keyboard. The program was presented with the Governor General's 
award at a special ceremony held in Quebec City.

With the literacy software developed by the Neil Squire Foundation 
and Capilano College, and speech synthesizer technology developed by 
engineers at  Digital, adult students can make use of specialized 
computer access devices and the DECtalk speech synthesizer units to 
read and write. 

Digital Equipment of Canada has donated more than $800,000 of the 
more $1 million worth of computer equipment, speech synthesizers and 
adaptative technologies donated over the last five years in support 
of the program. Other donors included The National Literacy 
Secretariat, British Columbia Ministry of  Education, Skills and 
Training, Ontario Training and Adjustment Board, Literacy Section, 
The J.W. McConnell Family Foundation, Vancouver Foundation and 
Microsoft Canada Inc. More than 200 SARAW systems have been made 
available in local literacy and community groups across Canada.

"One in five Canadians is functionally illiterate. The problem is 
compounded when physical disabilities make it difficult to speak 
clearly. For years these adults have felt the isolation and lack of 
personal power that comes with being unable to communicate," said 
Pat Hodgson, literacy instructor at Capilano College.

"With SARAW, adults unable to operate a standard computer keyboard 
can make use of special adaptive equipment to operate a computer, 
even if they are only able to control a single body movement, such 
as turning their head.  For these learners, SARAW is not simply 
another teaching tool, it is often the only way to interact in a 
literacy classroom, and with the rest of society," said Don Bentley, 
the Neil Squire Foundation's software development manager.

More than the ability to write, the DECtalk speech synthesizer units 
enable the computer system to read text aloud to students, as well 
as pronounce words the student has written. The unit allows the 
student to continually assess his or her input and verify whether or 
not it sounds correct.

"At Digital, we are proud to have been involved in such a rewarding 
project. Using the power of advanced technology, many Canadians now 
have a way to experience the joys of reading and writing," said Ron 
Thiel, program manager at Digital Equipment of Canada. "This has 
given many program participants a chance to write about their 
thoughts and feelings -- often for the very first time."

"Digital's involvement with SARAW is part of a worldwide 
philanthropic campaign. We felt SARAW was the best example of the 
kind of innovative work that is possible when private companies and 
non-profit agencies work together, and that spirit is still alive 
five years later," said Ron Catcheside, corporate communications 
manager at Digital Equipment of Canada. "Digital fundamentally 
believes that giving back to the communities in which our employees 
live and work is not only a responsibility, it's good business."

The Neil Squire Foundation was established in 1984. The foundation 
is a Canadian non-profit society whose purpose is to create 
opportunities for individuals who have significant physical 
disabilities. Through direct interaction with these individuals, the 
foundation researches, develops and delivers appropriate innovative 
services and technology to meet their needs. Based in Vancouver, the 
organization has a staff of 40 in four centres including Regina, 
Ottawa and Fredericton.
