                                              Digital Equipment Corporation
                                          Maynard, Massachusetts 01754-2571


                     Delivering on the Promise:
                    Digital's Strategy for Growth

                        Background Information

			 Digital's Mission...

		   Digital's mission is very clear:

                      Deliver with its partners
                    networked business solutions
                      based on high-performance
                       platforms and services


It speaks to what Digital does best -- high-performance networked
solutions -- as well as to the  customers it knows best, business
customers.  And it relies heavily on the partners who are so 
critical in providing value to its customers.  

Digital's ability to deliver networked business solutions was the
source of its success in the 1980s.  The difference now is that
instead of proprietary solutions, Digital is delivering some of the
most open solutions in the industry.  And Digital is doing it at a
time of growing customer demand for comprehensive, networked
solutions to complex, enterprise computing problems. 

Customers can buy platforms from an increasing number of vendors
today.  Very few companies can provide the platforms, the network
infrastructure, the applications, and the services -- including 
those from partners -- that make up a networked business solution.

The key to Digital's strategy is to identify the platforms and the
markets that offer the greatest opportunity for profitability and
growth.  Digital has identified three major growth platforms where 
it is focusing its investments:

    *  High-performance 64-bit UNIX platforms
    *  Windows NT throughout the enterprise, and
    *  Internet connectivity within and between enterprises

These are areas of technology transformation, where there is
significant potential for breakthrough growth and where Digital is
already differentiated from its competitors.

In addition to focusing on three growth platforms, Digital, by 
taking a targeted, selected marketing approach, intends to open new 
markets, drive resource alignment, improve sales productivity, focus 
customer selection and channel recruitment, and leverage the synergy 
between its systems and services business.  Digital recognizes it 
cannot be all things to all customers, and has set aggressive 
criteria for the markets in which it competes.  

Digital's investment criteria are crisp and specific.  It is 
targeting markets with rapid growth and the potential to generate 
substantial operating profit.  In each market, Digital also must 
have the potential to earn significant market share.  The 
corporation will not be satisfied with small market share in a large 
number of markets, as that is not a recipe for growth.  A 20 percent 
market share target says clearly that Digital plans to be one of the 
leaders in the markets it pursues, based on its growth platforms. 

Based on these criteria, Digital has identified nine high-growth
market segments to pursue.  

    *  Continuous Computing
    *  Data Warehousing
    *  Enterprise Applications
    *  Visual Computing
    *  Windows NT Integration
    *  Mail & Messaging
    *  Intranet
    *  Internet Commerce
    *  Internet Service Providers

The annual revenue opportunity presented by these markets is 
projected to be approximately $130 billion, or about 20 percent of 
the total Information Technology market by calendar year 2000.  
Digital is initiating a significant and deliberate focus of its 
resources and investment dollars toward delivering high-performance, 
networked solutions for these markets.  Digital, with its systems, 
software and service partners, intends to establish itself in these 
markets through deliberate and focused campaigns that are expected 
to yield substantial revenue growth and profit.

These markets do not cover all of Digital's businesses, or even all 
of its strategic businesses.  They represent key go-to-market 
initiatives and were selected based on the result of a thorough 
market analysis.  These are not technology-driven initiatives -- 
they are solution-driven.  They build on Digital-s strengths across 
its business units and leverage multiple technologies and services.  
They are also aligned with the three major growth platforms that 
Digital has identified as its technology focus.

Platform Strategy for Growth...

Digital's platform mission is to deliver the enterprise-class 
networked computing platforms to support network business solutions.  
What are the capabilities of an enterprise computing platform?

At the highest level, there are four:

    *  Reliability to support critical business processes.

    *  Capacity and scalability so that a deployed business solution 
       does not have to be discarded when requirements increase.

    *  Delivered application performance, rather than narrowly 
       defined benchmark performance.

    *  Integration with legacy systems, because a business 
       application rarely stands apart from existing business 
       applications.

These capabilities are provided by certain proprietary mainframe and 
minicomputer systems.  Digital's challenge is to deliver these four 
capabilities on platforms based on the new technology paradigms.  In 
September 1995, Digital shared its model of the four approaches to 
enterprise-level computing.  These approaches were so fundamentally 
different, Digital referred to them as paradigms: 

    *  Mainframes and minis as the past paradigm, though still 
       important to customers and vendors. 

    *  UNIX as the current dominant paradigm with the most new 
       investment.  Within UNIX, there was another emerging paradigm 
       shift to 64-bit computing.

    *  Windows NT as the emerging paradigm. 
 
    *  The Internet as another emerging paradigm, providing an 
       entirely new type of platform for a new class of enterprise 
       applications.

While many thought the Internet would lead to the downfall of
Microsoft and Windows NT, Digital thought the two would be
complementary or even convergent.

Digital believes these paradigms have become reality:
 
    *  64-bit computing in the form of 64-bit UNIX has emerged in 
       the marketplace.  Indeed, Digital has the leading position in 
       this market.

    *  Windows NT has emerged; the skeptics of a year ago are gone.

    *  And the Internet is emerging on such a broad front that its 
       impact will be significant.


Digital's platform strategy for growth is to exploit the emerging
paradigms.  The markets represented by these emerging paradigms:

    *  are characterized by rapid growth and little entrenched 
       competition

    *  are markets for which Digital has product and service 
       leadership

    *  and by their very nature, are markets based on emerging 
       paradigms can exploit the synergy between products and 
       service.

Digital's go-to-market strategy provides that synergy.

64-bit UNIX.  Digital's 64-bit UNIX strategy is very simple.  The
company pioneered 64-bit UNIX, started shipping it three years ago,
leads the market, and intends to maintain that lead.  Over the past 
three years Digital has secured a large set of 64-bit ISV
applications.  The combination of Digital UNIX and Alpha technology
consistently provides the best system performance at every price
point.  And for data-intensive applications that can exploit 64-bit
VLM and VLDB capabilities, Digital's 64-bit UNIX provides
breakthrough performance capabilities.

In commercial UNIX server applications, the client is invariably 
a Windows or Windows NT desktop.  By leveraging its Microsoft
technology expertise, Digital will consistently provide the 
leadership interoperability between Digital UNIX and Windows 
clients.  Digital believes its 64-bit UNIX provides leadership 
continuous computing. This results from combining UNIX and server 
technology with its global support services.

For 64-bit UNIX, Digital has targeted three go-to-market segments:  
Data Warehousing, Continuous Computing, and Enterprise Applications.
 
Windows NT.  Digital's Windows NT strategy is to lead in bringing
enterprise computing to Windows NT.  Digital was the first -- and to
date the only -- enterprise-class system vendor to really endorse 
Windows NT or enterprise computing. 

Because of the nature of Windows NT, Digital has fewer opportunities
than with UNIX, for a strategy strongly based only on product
differentiation.  Digital's differentiation in the market is based 
on a balance between products and services.  In support of Windows 
NT, Digital has by far the most comprehensive enterprise support and
integration services worldwide.  But also, in products, Digital does 
have significant differentiation for Windows NT.  

Digital is unique in offering dual hardware architecture  strategy 
supporting Windows NT which provides capacity and scalability not 
available from any other vendor.  Digital offers software products 
for manageability and cluster capabilities.  And finally, Digital's  
alliance with Microsoft is a major factor supporting its service, 
product, and marketing differentiation for Windows NT.

For Windows NT, Digital has targeted four go-to-market segments:  
Mail & Messaging, Windows NT Integration, Visual Computing, and 
Enterprise Applications.

Internet.  Digital's Internet strategy is to bring business 
computing to the Internet.  As with Windows NT, this strategy is 
based on a balance between product and service differentiation.  
Digital leads in a  number of product areas.  It has by far the 
highest-performance Internet servers in the market, as measured by 
the standard Webstone and SPECweb96 benchmarks.  It leads in 
Internet protocol-switching technology, and has an innovative 
AltaVista software portfolio for security, information access, and 
collaboration.  For services leadership, Digital offers the 
industry's most comprehensive Internet services and solutions 
portfolio. 

For the Internet platform, Digital has focused on three go-to-market 
segments:  Intranets, Internet Commerce, and Internet Service 
Providers. 

Go-to-Market Strategy for Growth...

The go-to-market strategy is focused on new opportunities in 
high-growth markets where Digital is well positioned with its 
technology, expertise, and alliances.  Digital delivers to these 
markets repeatable solutions which can be customized by industry, 
company size, and integrated into the existing computing 
environment.

The world is rapidly becoming a place where anyone, anywhere, can 
gain immediate access to information.  This creates new 
opportunities for businesses to become part of virtual networked 
enterprises' intimately linked with customers and partners.  New 
Internet and enterprise applications hold the promise of reducing 
computing and communication costs.  They present the opportunity to 
build cohesive infrastructures to support day-to-day operations.  At 
the same time, these new networked solutions can provide customers 
with the information they need to manage change and implement 
corporate initiatives that will deliver greater value to their 
customers and shareholders. 

Digital's growth strategy focuses on nine key markets where 
customers are looking for business solutions that will enable them 
to create their networked enterprise.  These solutions are built on 
its expertise in Internet connectivity, high-performance 64-bit UNIX 
and the implementation of Windows NT across the enterprise.  These 
key markets include:

Continuous Computing.  High availability is critical to customers 
who rely on their information technology systems for 
mission-critical services.  Digital provides the widest range of 
performance-driven continuous computing solutions, based on 
clustering technology, that are affordable and manageable.  These 
solutions are backed by global planning, implementation, and support 
services.  Quite simply, Digital defined the market for clustering.  
Digital has leveraged its leadership and experience in installing 
more than 65,000 clusters for the OpenVMS environment and has 
focused these core competencies in creating the best UNIX and 
Windows NT cluster solutions in the market.  Customers benefit from 
no trade-offs, and breakthrough cluster functionality that provides 
a continuum of availability for the UNIX, OpenVMS, and Windows NT 
environments. 

Data Warehousing. Customers are looking for ways to manage one of 
their most important corporate assets -- information.  Digital and 
its partners enable customers to transform data into information 
customers can use for competitive advantage.  Digital already has 
the products, services, and partnerships to deliver leadership 
warehousing solutions.  In fact, Alpha is the fastest-growing system 
for data warehousing solutions. 

The uniqueness Digital brings to the data warehousing market is its 
complete 64-bit solution consisting of 64-bit hardware, operating 
system and database.  This is the only complete 64-bit solution on 
the market, and it drives the performance and scalability required 
by data warehousing applications.  Furthermore, Digital solutions 
are built from open, standard components with no exotic technology 
like MPP.  Tuned applications running on Alpha systems deliver up to 
200 times faster performance than on 32-bit systems. 

Digital will continue to deliver superior technology as an 
application enabler.  It will strengthen and leverage high-impact 
partner relationships with companies like Oracle and Red Brick, and 
leverage global data warehouse delivery and support capabilities. 

Enterprise applications.  Customers are continuously under pressure 
to respond to their enterprise needs  for applications that are 
highly available, deployable globally with delivered performance, 
have minimal risk of failure, and can be managed by a continuously 
leaner set of resources, in terms of people and budget. 

Digital leads the competition in understanding how to tune high-end 
enterprise applications to address these customers needs.  It has 
emerged as a significant player in the enterprise applications 
market through partnerships with leading independent software 
vendors including SAP, Baan, PeopleSoft, Oracle, and Computer 
Associates. Today, these software applications are experiencing 
unparalleled performance and availability on Digital's 64-bit UNIX 
platforms. Digital knows how to turn technology advantage into 
applications benefits.  

Digital is bringing this experience to the Windows NT platform as 
leading applications are being rapidly migrated in response to 
customer demand.  Digital also helps enterprise customers develop, 
deploy, manage, migrate, and integrate enterprise applications more 
quickly, completely, and cost-effectively than any other vendor. 

Visual Computing.  There are many businesses that rely on 
visualization and compute power to accelerate the creative process, 
leading to competitive advantage.  

Digital brings to customers the industry's highest-performance 
visual computing environments with leading-edge Windows NT solutions 
on Alpha and Intel architectures.  Digital solutions are driving 
down the overall cost of ownership.  They deliver proven performance 
to significantly improve the creativity and time-to-market for 
customers whose own products are the result of the creative process.

Over the past year, Digital has successfully gained share with 
independent software vendors who are dependent on the performance of 
Alpha for delivering competitive advantage to their customers. 
Digital's commitment to Alpha technology ensures that it will be 
positioned as the leader in performance, while its strength in 
Windows NT solutions will offer customers superior price/performance 
advantages.

Windows NT Integration.  The migration to Windows NT-based 
client-server computing reflects the ongoing trend in enterprise 
environments towards open systems and standards, and an IT strategy 
aligned with business processes.  Enterprise-wide integration of 
Windows NT demands interoperability with heterogeneous systems. 

Digital offers the most comprehensive NT-integration solutions in 
the market today.  Based on a proven three-tier client-server model, 
Digital's Windows NT integration solutions enable customers to write 
software that seamlessly integrates desktop, department, and data 
center applications and data.  The solution set includes portable 
middleware, common network infrastructure, 64-bit Alpha systems, 
Intel systems, storage hardware, and management products.  
Complementing this product set are tools from many independent 
software vendors. Within a year, Digital expects to have 1,500 
Windows NT support engineers, the largest Windows NT professional 
services workforce in the world.  It delivers unparalleled planning, 
design, implementation and management services for bringing Windows 
NT to the enterprise. 

Mail & Messaging.  A reliable, secure, and efficient messaging 
infrastructure is critical to meeting the electronic communication, 
collaboration, and transaction needs within and between enterprises. 
The need to integrate electronic messaging into key business 
processes and provide seamless connectivity via the Internet 
requires a robust and high-performance messaging infrastructure.  
Consequently, a number of enterprises are migrating from their 
existing legacy and LAN-based mail systems to solutions capable of 
meeting their current and future needs. 

Digital provides worldwide enterprise mail and messaging leadership 
today.  Digital offers high performance, high availability and 
scaleable messaging solutions based on Microsoft Exchange, Digital 
platforms, and value-added software and services.  Digital has 
integrated its AltaVista Mail, MAILBus 400, X.500 directory 
services, and ALL-IN-1 product set with Microsoft Exchange to enable 
complete integration with existing messaging infrastructures. 

Digital and its partners provide a full set of modular services to 
support the range of activities that customers need in deploying 
enterprise messaging.  More than 700 Digital service professionals 
are dedicated to providing Windows NT/Exchange solutions worldwide.  
Today they are delivering professional services that include 
technical consulting, architectural design, technology selection, 
solutions implementation and integration, and life-cycle maintenance 
and support. 

Intranet.  The term "intranet" has been used to refer to Internet 
technology working over a private corporate network.  But companies 
are finding this view too narrow.  Two key areas of opportunity are 
missed in this restricted view:  new business solutions need to 
integrate with legacy systems; and extensions to the Internet make 
it possible for businesses to conduct secure, high-bandwidth 
transactions over the public Internet infrastructure.  These 
opportunities expand the view past the traditional boundaries of the 
private corporate network. 

Digital's intranet strategy is to enable enterprises to use Internet 
technologies to expand their market reach, vastly increase 
intra-company productivity, and securely connect and collaborate 
with their virtual organizations.  Digital is the only IT vendor 
capable of meeting the demands of global intranet computing.  
Digital has the highest-performance Internet servers with continuous 
computing capabilities.  It has a rich heritage in networking and 
today leads the market in high-performance switching and Network 
integration services.  Its compelling portfolio of AltaVista 
software provides customers with the tools to quickly find useful 
information, including legacy data.  Digital also provides the 
platform software to easily communicate and collaborate with 
distributed teams over a secure virtual network.  These capabilities 
are delivered by a service organization that has been a leader in 
designing, planning, implementing and managing networked solutions 
for more than 15 years. 

Internet commerce.  Digital believes the Internet commerce market 
will experience explosive growth over the next five years.  
Customers will adopt a broad range of new tools and technologies for 
Internet commerce applications.  Today, commerce applications are 
focused primarily on functionality and ease of  use.  Today's 
solutions do not yet provide the mission-critical capabilities of 
existing transactional systems, especially in the areas of 
availability, security, and integrity.

This is where Digital is focusing its efforts.  Its intention is to 
be the market leader in solutions for the most demanding 
business-to-business and business-to-consumer applications.  Digital 
and its partners will deliver end-to-end transaction integrity for 
customers doing business over the Internet.

Today, Digital provides outstanding security software, from 
firewalls to tunnels, combined with a full range of services to 
support the emerging Internet commerce applications.  Digital's 
combined strengths in services, networking, production OLTP and 
Internet security, leveraged with a rich partner portfolio and 
solution-focused organization, will make it a major player in 
Internet commerce. Digital will deliver six integrated solution sets 
to selected sub-markets. 

Future directions include close parallel development with strategic
partners; introducing breakthrough technology such as "Millicent" 
for micro-commerce, and co-location of major electronic commerce 
players at Digital's Internet Exchange.

Internet Service Providers.  Internet Services Providers, under 
intense competition, need to manage the phenomenal subscriber 
growth, provide 100 percent availability, and constantly lower the 
cost of service and capital.  This drives their purchase decisions.   

Digital's strategy is to be the leader in production-quality ISP 
solutions.  It intends to grow share in the UNIX-based market while 
working closely with Microsoft to establish leadership in the 
Windows NT-based ISP solutions and related services.  Digital's 
solution portfolio for Internet Service Providers affords high 
performance, availability, and scalability, with support for major 
Internet software on Alpha.  It has pre-tested Alpha and Intel 
solutions to meet the extraordinary demands for rapid time-to-market 
for the new Internet Service Providers.  Digital's high-availability 
data-switching and IP-switching products are a critical component of 
an ISP solution.  The Digital Services Division offers a full range 
of offerings from applications assessment to customer solutions 
development and integration. 

To provide the entire life-cycle of services, Digital in July opened 
the Digital Internet Exchange, the Internet's first combined 
switching and commercial data center.  The exchange offers Internet 
service providers and their customers mission-critical "lights-out" 
operation, a full range of Internet services, and a choice of 
telecommunications carriers.  No other commercial exchange or 
computer vendor provides this combination.

Strategic Alliances with Industry Leaders...

Digital is teaming with industry-leading software and services 
companies in major strategic agreements for joint engineering and 
development, marketing plans and implementations, and sales 
engagements.  The resulting products, services and support will 
complement the participants own offerings and leverage market 
penetration for all.

The Microsoft/Digital "Alliance for Enterprise Computing," now in 
its second year, combines both companies strengths and resources to 
solve complex customer problems.  The alliance focuses on helping 
customers develop, deploy, manage, migrate and integrate 
Windows-based solutions more quickly, completely and 
cost-effectively than any other vendor. 

The Digital/Oracle "Alliance for Enterprise Databases," built on 
nearly 20 years of successful business collaboration, provides 
industry-leading software running on the world's fastest computer 
systems.  These  capabilities deliver the performance and power 
customers demand for complex, challenging applications.  These 
include Very Large Memory/Very Large Database data warehousing 
solutions.

The unprecedented Digital/MCI/Microsoft "Alliance for Intranet 
Computing" leverages the combined strengths of the participants to 
offer packaged Intranet services; the first integrated end-to-end 
enterprise solutions for managing enterprise mail services; an 
information utility to deliver fully integrated services; and 
one-stop shopping for hardware, software and networking solutions 
and support.  This alliance builds on the Microsoft/Digital Alliance 
for Enterprise Computing and the MCI/Microsoft partnership for the 
Microsoft Network.

The Digital/Computer Associates "Alliance for Enterprise Management"
delivers comprehensive enterprise management solutions to customers. 
Comp brings leading "mission-critical" enterprise management 
software; and Digital brings its global reach of award-winning 
enterprise deployment, services, support and systems integration 
along with leading client/server platforms for availability, 
scalability and performance.  Customers gain the ability to rapidly 
integrate 64-bit UNIX, Windows NT, and Internet platforms into their 
enterprise. 

These relationships and others enable Digital and its partners to 
address the market reality that no single information technology 
company can provide all enterprise solutions to all customers. 
Strategic partnerships, technology sharing, joint product 
development and marketing, complementary products, services and 
support, are required to succeed in today's rapidly changing 
computing environment.

Financial Strength...

Key financial indicators demonstrate that Digital's core strategy, 
focused on three primary growth platforms, is delivering.  In the 
past two years, the company achieved a $1 billion improvement in 
profits from operations, resulting from a 13 percent and then a 16 
percent increase in product revenue year over year.

Alpha revenues grew 45 percent last year, driven by increasing 
demand for AlphaServer 8000 family systems and, more recently, by 
growing demand for the new midrange AlphaServer 4100 system servers.  
Both families offer unprecedented performance and price/performance.

Digital UNIX system revenues continue to grow -- up more than 50 
percent year over year -- on a base that is expanding every year. 
Most of that growth is due to new applications or new customers, 
further indicators that Digital's platform strategy is paying off.

Windows NT is achieving significant market penetration.  Digital's 
growing base more than doubled last fiscal year in this critically 
important segment.

Capitalizing on Digital's AltaVista technology and high performance 
GIGAswitch products, and strong heritage in networking integration 
services, the company is building strong and growing businesses in 
three targeted growth markets:  Intranet, Internet Commerce, and 
Internet Service Providers.  

OpenVMS systems, continuously upgraded in features, functionality, 
and robustness, are providing a secure operating environment for 
Alpha and VAX systems.  These systems are showing stable revenues 
with the decline in VAX systems revenue offset by the growth on 
Alpha Systems.  

Intel servers, network and storage products, and multivendor 
services all posted at least 30 percent growth rates as well, clear 
signals that Digital is gaining strength.

Total operating revenue was $14.6 billion, up 5 percent over FY95, 
or 9 percent when adjusted for divestments.  This was the highest 
level of revenue in the company's history, despite the fact that 
Digital has divested businesses during the past two years that 
generated more than $1 billion dollars of annual revenue. 

Digital's balance sheet strengthened this past fiscal year, despite 
a restructuring charge in June 1996.  Two billion dollars in cash, 
the highest reserve level the company has had in five years, and low 
debt for an industrial company its size, ensure that Digital can 
afford investments in products, sales, and services required to 
deliver networked business solutions and to grow its business.

Winning strategy...

Digital is poised once again, as it was with distributed computing 
and the 32-bit VAX architecture in the 1980s, to lead where the 
market is heading.  Digital has a focused mission-clear strategy for 
growth...leadership technology and services...an aggressive 
go-to-market plan, and a strong financial position to support its 
strategic businesses.

It also has another critical asset -- talented and dedicated 
employees.  Their loyalty, commitment to  innovation, "whatever it 
takes" spirit, and perseverance during challenging times have been 
crucial to Digital's turnaround.  And it is their hard work and 
determination that will drive Digital's long-term success.  
