            Leading Operating System, Embedded Tool Vendors
                Endorse StrongARM Microprocessor Family


     ...Customer Demand Drives Third-Party Support for StrongARM...


SAN JOSE, California, September 17, 1996 -- Key operating system and 
application development tool vendors today announced their support for 
Digital Equipment Corporation's StrongARM microprocessor family.  Made 
at the Embedded Systems Conference '96 in the Convention Center, the 
announcements signal the growing momentum of StrongARM development 
activity among embedded system designers and software providers.  

New Product Generations

"This rising tide of support for StrongARM from the industry's leading 
realtime operating system and tool suppliers is evidence of the 
emergence of StrongARM as the processor of choice for today's 
performance-critical embedded applications," said Kevin Fielding, 
StrongARM marketing manager, Digital Semiconductor.

"These are the most popular tools in use today, and cover the broad 
spectrum of today's embedded applications.  Along with other special 
feature tools, they  provide efficient vehicles for system developers to 
port their applications to the StrongARM architecture.  They also enable 
our customers to deliver superior products with the StrongARM SA-110, 
including our new 233MHz and 166MHz versions," he continued.

Vendors Voice Support

David Larrimore, vice president of marketing for Wind River Systems, 
said, "Deeply embedded products require a cost-sensitive development 
solution that can offer high performance with low cost.  The StrongARM 
architecture is designed specifically to meet these requirements.  The 
availability of Wind River's award-winning Tornado development 
environment for ARM devices will enable developers to bring these 
products to market quickly and with minimal risk."

"Cygnus is pleased to announce support of Digital Semiconductor's 
StrongARM processor as part of our GNUPro Developer's Kit for the ARM 
family," stated Sayan Chakraborty, director of business development at 
Cygnus Support.  "We feel that StrongARM is uniquely positioned to 
enable high-performance, low-cost solutions.  Customers developing such 
complex, time-to-market and cost-critical products are also those who 
have traditionally moved toward a GNU tool solution backed by Cygnus."

According to Robin Saxby, president and CEO of Advanced RISC Machines 
Ltd., "The first implementation of StrongARM, from Digital, is gaining 
great favor with the high-performance embedded community and is an 
example of how our partnership provides the best solutions for 
customers.  The new version of our ARM Software Developers' Toolkit 
contains StrongARM device-specific components that speed the development 
process for a variety of embedded and consumer applications."

Application-Specific and Special Feature Tools

Besides working with vendors of the widely accepted, broad-based tools 
announced today, Digital is cooperating with additional vendors to offer 
more application-focused tools.  Developers of products such as Internet 
appliances and "smart" handheld devices need scalable, embedded 
operating systems with specialty features and system services.

Internet appliance designers can take advantage of JavaSoft's JavaOS, 
Acorn Risc Technologies' RISC OS, and Oracle's NCOS operating systems on 
StrongARM.  Designers of "smart" handheld devices such as Personal 
Digital Assistants (PDAs) can count on the support of Psion's Epoc-32 
and Apple Computer's Newton 2.0 OS.  Interactive multimedia vendors can 
look to ISI's pSOSystem software as well as Wind River's Tornado 
development environment.

"I'm a great fan of the StrongARM port," said Jim Mitchell, Sun Fellow 
and vice president for technology and architecture at JavaSoft.  "We're 
delighted to see Java reaching a broad new group of embedded system 
designers and software providers through this JavaOS port.  With JavaOS, 
we expect to see Java become a ubiquitous feature of every architecture, 
and StrongARM's momentum will go a long way toward ensuring that."

John Hogan, director of core products marketing at Integrated Systems, 
Inc., said, "The StrongARM processor, with its high performance, power 
efficiency and low price, is a natural platform for applications in 
interactive multimedia, communications, and embedded Internet, where our 
pSOSystem embedded operating system and pRISM+ development environment 
are strong.  We are pleased to work with Digital Semiconductor to 
provide to system manufacturers a powerful embedded platform with an 
application-rich environment."

"We are moving quickly to add more applications and development support 
for StrongARM in order to help speed our customers' 'time-to-revenue'," 
Digital's Fielding said.  "With today's announcement, we can offer the 
tools that developers of products such as Internet terminals, PDAs, 
network routers, and other communications devices need -- scalable 
operating systems, state-of-the-art development tools, embedded 
networking support, and fully featured system services."

Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and multimedia, 
plus low-power StrongARM microprocessors under license from Advanced 
RISC Machines Ltd.  Mitsubishi Electric Corporation and Samsung 
Electronics Company Ltd. are alternate sources for Alpha 
microprocessors.  World Wide Web site:  

	http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor

Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/server 
solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide information 
systems.  Digital's Alpha and Intel platforms, storage, networking, 
software and services, together with industry-focused solutions from 
business partners, help organizations compete and win in today's global 
marketplace.
