                  Digital, VLSI Technology Announce Agreement
                  for Alpha Core Logic Chip Design, Production


      ...Supports Alpha's Move Into Corporate Mainstream Desktop Market...


MAYNARD, Mass., September 16, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation today 
announced an agreement with VLSI Technology, Inc., for the design and 
manufacture of core logic chips for use with future models of Digital's Alpha
microprocessors.  The agreement signals the beginning of a long-term, 
cooperative relationship between the two companies to produce and market Alpha 
components and subsystems to OEM system manufacturers.

    System logic components connect microprocessors to memory, the PCI data 
bus, and peripheral functions such as audio, graphics, input devices, and I/O 
ports.  System logic chips developed by VLSI under the agreement will help 
Alpha-based computers to achieve their full performance potential
and aid Alpha's migration into the corporate mainstream desktop market.

    "VLSI Technology has established an excellent reputation for quality and 
performance in the support logic chip market," said William N. Johnson, Digital 
Semiconductor's vice president, Semiconductor Business Segments.  "This
agreement supports our strategy of moving Alpha into the corporate mainstream 
desktop market.  It ensures an adequate supply of high-performance core logic 
chips to meet an anticipated growing demand in the Windows PC industry for
Alpha microprocessors and Alpha-based systems."

    Umesh Padval, VLSI senior vice president for computing products, commented, 
"The technology cooperation between Digital Semiconductor and VLSI demonstrates 
how VLSI's tailored approach to system logic for advanced microprocessors adds
compelling performance differentiation to all kinds of computing platforms.  
Through this agreement, we aim to deliver solutions that will give end users 
the full measure of performance available through the Alpha microprocessor."

    VLSI Technology, Inc. designs and manufactures System-Level Silicon (tm) 
solutions based on its library of FSB (tm) functional system blocks.  Targeting 
its offerings toward the communications, consumer digital entertainment, and 
computing markets, the company offers its customers advanced system-level 
integration capabilities.  The company is based in San Jose, California, with 
1995 revenues of $720 million, and approximately 3,000 employees worldwide.
Visit VLSI's homepage at http://www.vlsi.com.

    Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and markets 
industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha microprocessors and PCI 
chips for networking, bridging, and multimedia, plus low-power StrongARM 
microprocessors under license from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd.  Mitsubishi 
Electric Corporation and Samsung Electronics Company Ltd. are alternate sources 
for Alpha microprocessors.  Web site: http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor

    Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/server 
solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide information systems.  
Digital's Alpha and Intel platforms, storage, networking, software and
services, together with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
