                 Digital Wins "Business On The Internet" Awards
                            For Intranet Excellence


Networld + InterOp '96, Atlanta, Georgia -- September 18, 1996 -- At 
ceremonies held at Networld + InterOp 1996, Digital Equipment Corporation was 
honored with receiving two Business on the Internet (BOTI) Awards presented by 
CMP Publications and SOFTBANK Expos, recognizing Digital as an innovative
organization that uses the Internet and Intranet technologies to create
distinct business advantages. These first ever BOTI Awards were presented to
Digital for two categories: The Most Comprehensive Intranet and Best Use of
Leading Edge Technology.

     "The remarkable, symmetrical growth of both the Internet and Intranet 
demand new ways of thinking. Simply setting up a corporate Web site simply isn't
adequate anymore. These first BOTI Awards were designed to single out those
companies who dared to push the envelope. They recognize companies that view
technology as a tool for solving the toughest challenges in the enterprise,"
said Patricia Schnaidt, Network Computing's Editor-in-Chief. Mitch Irsfeld,
Editor-in-Chief of CommunicationsWeek concurred. "This year's BOTI Award
winners have set the early standards, scratching the surface of what will be
possible in the years ahead. As the Internet and Intranet become staples in
businesses around the world, these organizations will be looked up to as
examples of the best in American business innovation."

     The Most Comprehensive Intranet Award was presented to acknowledge
Digital's internal Intranet implementation. This includes Virtual Private
Networks (VPN) to extend the Internet to over 9,000 mobile sales reps and
home-based workers worldwide. This Intranet operates on all of Digital's
networking equipment including DEChub 900 MultiSwitches, DECNIS routers, and
the high-performing GIGAswitch/FDDI, for access to local Internet Service
Providers (ISPs). 

     "One of our key challenges was to seamlessly move our home-based employees 
from the 1-800 phone network and legacy database platform to the Internet. The
connections needed to be as if they were actually physically sitting in one of
our facilities," said Ginny Covino, Director of Business Systems & Tools, for
Digital's Systems Business Unit. "We not only offer these services to our
employees, Digital is a leader in providing similar Intranet solutions to our
customers."

     The Best Use of Leading Edge Technology was awarded to Digital for its
open, multiplatform Intranet implementation, including high-performance 64-bit
UNIX and Intel server hardware and server software from Microsoft, Netscape,
and Purveyor. Digital's Intranet also implements the AltaVista search engine
and AltaVista Forum; audio and video capability; Java and ActiveX applets; a
centralized 64-bit document database and automated reader profiling capability.
Finally, the Digital Intranet also leverages legacy data while using AltaVista
tunnels and password protected areas. 

     "Our Intranet is critical to our organization because it provides a wealth
of information that enables the employees and partners to make informed
business decisions. As well, Digital reaps the benefits of cost economies,
increased productivity, and increased customer satisfaction," said Kathleen
Warner, Digital's Director, Internet Program Office. "That information also
means that the Intranet is Digital's University." 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/server
solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide information systems.
Digital's scaleable Alpha and Intel platforms, storage, networking, software
and services, together with industry-focused solutions from business partners,
help organizations compete and win in today's global markeplace. For more
information, visit Digital's World Wide Web site at http://www.digital.com. 

