MAYNARD, Mass., September 23, 1996 -- Extending the company's lead 
in delivering Microsoft Windows NT solutions across the enterprise, 
Digital Equipment Corporation today announced new Pentium Pro-based 
Personal Workstations and high-performance StorageWorks solutions -- 
all optimized for the Windows NT operating system. Today's 
announcement further delivers on Digital's Windows NT strategy to 
lead the industry in bringing enterprise computing to Windows NT.

     Digital's new Personal Workstations deliver performance 
competitive with traditional UNIX workstations at a fraction of the 
cost, while StorageWorks NT Cluster Kits are specifically designed 
to support mission critical applications in production environments, 
which demand high availability, high performance and high 
reliability.

     "Digital is leading the Windows NT revolution," said Harry 
Copperman, vice president and general manager of Digital's Systems 
Business Unit. "While Hewlett-Packard, Compaq and others are 
scrambling just to get in the personal workstation business, we're 
delivering to our customers next-generation personal workstations, 
the industry's only Windows NT-based systems upgradeable to a 
powerful RISC architecture - Digital's Alpha.  And the combination 
of Digital Intel- and Alpha-based clusters for Windows NT with 
StorageWorks products provides an ideal solution for high 
performance storage and high availability in a Windows NT 
environment."

     "Digital's Personal Workstations, available as single- and 
multiprocessor deskside systems, fuel the growing volume market for 
Windows NT workstations and boost Digital's lead in bringing Windows 
NT to corporate and technical computing environments," said Philippe 
Ribeyre, vice president of Digital's Workstation Business Segment.  
"With Digital's leadership Windows NT expertise, our unique 
relationship with Microsoft through the Alliance for Enterprise 
Computing and support from key application vendors, it's clear that 
Digital is best positioned to lead the Windows NT revolution."

     Personal Workstations feature the fastest Pentium Pro power, 
Digital's new Personal Workstations incorporate the fastest Pentium 
Pro microprocessors, multiprocessing capability, a range of new 3D 
graphics options, faster components, and advanced integration and 
expandability. The Personal Workstations are designed for customers 
with graphics-intensive application needs in the MCAD, EDA, 
animation, entertainment, financial, geographic information systems 
and software engineering markets.

     Benchmark tests using technical applications such as EDS 
Unigraphics and Parametric Technology Corporation's Pro/ENGINEER 
demonstrate the price/performance superiority of Digital's Personal 
Workstation 200i over the competition. The new workstation provides 
comparable performance at one- half to one-third the cost of 
Hewlett-Packard's C110, Silicon Graphics, Inc.'s Indigo2 R4400, 
and Sun Microsystem's Ultra 170 workstations.

     "Digital's Personal Workstations redefine the cost structure of 
traditional mid- and entry-level workstations," said Andy Feit, 
analyst at Dataquest.

     Personal Workstation customers can take advantage of Digital's 
investment-protecting PowerGrade processor upgrade, which enables a 
"do-it-yourself" upgrade to more powerful Intel and Digital Alpha 
processors as they become available.

     Frank Foster, vice president of pre-visualization and 
multimedia at Sony Pictures Imageworks, uses Digital's Personal 
Workstations for the design and creation of motion pictures and was 
an early beta user. "We tested our applications on Digital's 
Personal Workstation 200i and were delighted with the overall 
performance and design of the machine," he said. "We were also glad 
to know we can upgrade the workstation with an Alpha processor in 
the future."

     Digital Personal Workstations are available with either Windows 
NT version 3.51 or version 4.0 factory-installed. The systems 
feature Intel's 440FX Pentium Pro chipset and support from 32 to 256 
megabytes of EDO memory. For ECC support, configurations are 
available with 64 to 512 megabytes of Fast Page memory.  Also 
featured are an Ultrawide SCSI disk controller, 2-gigabyte hard 
drive, 8x EIDE CD-ROM drive, a full range of 3-D workstation 
graphics options including Digital's latest PowerStorm OpenGL 
adapters, integrated 16-bit audio and integrated Ethernet.

     The Personal Workstation Model 200i, based on the 200MHz 
Pentium Pro, is priced from US$5,372 fully configured. Base systems 
- -- without hard drive and graphics adapter -- are available for 
resellers requiring a building-block approach. The 180MHz Model 180I 
base system is priced at $3,697. The Model 200i base system is 
priced at $3,792. The Model 200i2 base system, with dual 200MHz 
processors, is priced at $4,751. All Personal Workstations feature 
a three-year warranty, with the first year on site.

     Personal Workstations are available immediately from Digital's 
channel partners.  Digital has embraced an all-channels strategy for 
its Personal Workstations, ensuring availability from a wide range 
of distributors worldwide. For more information on Digital's 
Personal Workstations visit http://www.workstation.digital.com  on 
the World Wide Web.

     StorageWorks NT Clusters Kits maximizes customers' investments 
The StorageWorks NT Cluster Kits provide customers with the benefits 
of high availability storage in Windows NT cluster configurations. 
Customers now can easily implement clusters for Windows NT on top of 
RAID configurations.  Available for Digital's 64-bit AlphaServer and 
Intel-based Prioris servers, StorageWorks NT Clusters Kits provide 
the storage infrastructure to maximize Windows NT cluster 
investments.

     The new StorageWorks NT Cluster Kits provide the highest levels 
of pre-Configuration and integration in the Windows NT cluster 
market. Customers running Windows NT applications that see data and 
application availability as critical to the success of their 
business can now rely on Digital's coupling of clustering and RAID 
technologies.  Examples of these customers include those making a 
strategic commitment to Windows NT, PC LAN computing environments 
that require high availability and businesses that have delayed the 
deployment of business-critical applications on Windows NT until 
RAID storage subsystems are supported. StorageWorks NT Cluster Kits 
are available immediately and range in price from $499 for the 
StorageWorks cluster enabling kit to $3,479 for the Windows NT/Alpha 
Clusters Kit.  All Cluster Kits support the RAID Array 310 and the 
Raid Array 410 storage subsystem.  The RAID Array 310 is a 
standalone solution for users in medium-sized departments or large 
workgroups and supports 10-60GB of storage capacity.  The RAID 410 
offers comprehensive support for client/server and mission critical 
applications for small data centers or large department 
environments.  It supports up to 103GB of storage.

     Customers requiring more information about Digital's complete 
line of storage hardware and software products could call 
1-800-STORWORK (1-800-786-7967) or visit 
http://www.storage.digital.com on the World Wide Web.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is a world leader in open client/
server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide 
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha and Intel platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
