             ISDN PC Desktop Video Conference Highlights
            Digital Semiconductor Exhibit at DVC '96 East


           ...Shows Standards Compliance, Ease of Delivery,
                        High Image Quality...


BOSTON, September 4, 1996 -- PC-based desktop video conferencing, 
using Digital Equipment Corporation's 21230 video codec chip in an 
Integrated Services Data Network (ISDN) environment, is being 
demonstrated this week at DVC '96 East, the desktop video-
conferencing conference and exposition held today through Friday 
at the Sheraton Boston Hotel and Towers.

     The live video conference, at Booth 712 of Digital 
Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business, 
demonstrates ITV-T H.320 standard compliance for ISDN video 
conferencing with two Pentium desktop PCs.  The Pentium systems run 
Windows 95 software and each contains a 21230 video codec 
(coder-decoder) board and an ISDN card.  Digital Semiconductor's 
21230 chip performs H.261-compliant video compression algorithms.  
An Analog Devices 2181 digital signal processor (DSP) handles audio 
compression.  A pre-release version of Digital's DSmediaPhone 
conferencing application software establishes and manages the 
connection.

     A presentation of 21230 video conferencing technology will be 
given by Frank Schapfel, Digital Semiconductor's multimedia 
marketing manager, today at 5:30 p.m. in the Exeter Room.

Solution Promotes H.320 Standard

     "As a corporate sponsor of DVC '96, this is Digital 
Semiconductor's first foray into the digital video conferencing 
market," said Schapfel.  "We are promoting adoption of the H.320 
standard and showing how easy it is to deliver a H.320 solution with 
the 21230 chip.  Our implementation is ideal for business users who 
need video conferencing capability in a Windows environment.  It is 
interoperable with other implementations, including H.320 desktops 
and H.320-based room or mobile video conferencing facilities."

     The 21230 video codec chip features very high image quality 
with full Common Interchange Format (CIF) resolution, delivering 15 
frames per second in full duplex (simultaneous send-receive) 
transmission.

Evaluation Board, VC Software

     The 21230 chip is currently shipping at a price of $75 in 
quantities of 10,000.  An evaluation board for OEMs is also 
available for $995.  Video conferencing software will be available 
in the fourth quarter of 1996 as part of the evaluation board driver 
set, which includes Windows 95 drivers, compression algorithms, and 
DSmediaPhone software.

